Share native members through lazy tree mappings

Native transport should preserve shared relation suffixes when member
images follow an increasing partial map. Carry compressed offset runs
through prefix keys and suffix values, compose compatible offsets, and
cache transformed children only when they are visited.

Keep small materialized relations eager and merge non-injective images
into owned destination suffixes. Represent dense leaves as intervals
and retain persistent interval trees when equality introduces holes.
Consume incoming suffixes during insertion so fresh pivots stay unique
through recursive unions instead of being copied again.

Preserve canonical current rows, historical snapshots, and finite fuel
through equality and updates. Keep cache coordinates tied to their raw
source and release deep mapping chains without recursive stack growth.
Use partial domains only to choose comparison lookups; leave stored
bounds and eager-mapping thresholds unchanged. Equivalent partial views
still skip their shared source without visiting individual keys.

Keep ordinary nodes compact, update only edited paths, and build sorted
levels without repeated searches. Specialize exact-tree updates to skip
deferred-node checks and adjust known counts directly. Keep the small
balance check inline while resolving rotations in a separate helper.
Reuse target coverage during ownership cleanup and share transport map
deltas only when both current and previous snapshots match.

Regression coverage includes native and desugared evaluators under both
evaluation strategies, randomized mutation sequences, filtered pivots,
partial and conflicting images, noncanonical rows, source and owner
equalities, shared snapshots, deep chains, late images, and finite fuel.

Record native CGU16 measurements against f29c6e6. Dense consumption
improves substantially; sparse incremental and equality controls still
have measurable overhead. Preserve the exact methodology and hashes.

Lazy caches and stack-safe destruction tighten the generic WBTreeMap
lifetime behavior: previous covariance and some early-dropping borrowed
value examples are not preserved. Generated evaluators use owned values.
